{"task_id": "HumanEval/99", "solution": "import math\ndef closest_integer(value: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Converts a string to float and rounds to the closest integer.\n    Numbers equidistant from two integers (X.5) are rounded away from zero.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        num_float = float(value)\n    except ValueError:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Invalid input: '{value}' is not a valid number string.\")\n\n    # Calculate the fractional part to identify .5 cases.\n    fractional_part = num_float - math.trunc(num_float)\n\n    if abs(fractional_part) == 0.5:\n        # Round away from zero for exact .5 cases.\n        if num_float >= 0:\n            return math.ceil(num_float)\n        else: # num_float < 0\n            return math.floor(num_float)\n    else:\n        # Use standard rounding for other fractional parts.\n        return round(num_float)"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/100", "solution": "def make_a_pile(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the number of stones for n levels, where the first level has n stones and subsequent levels increase by 2.\n    Returns a list of integers representing stones per level.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    pile_levels = [n]\n    current_stones = n\n\n    for _ in range(1, n):\n        current_stones += 2\n        pile_levels.append(current_stones)\n\n    return pile_levels"}
